speechtext:
My recollection is that this provision was recommended by Sir George Murray, but whether recommended by him or not I think it is wise legislation if the
280}
Dominion is to carry on efficiently its business throughout the country. Provision is also made in the new legislation for control of the employment of temporary clerks and for regulating holiday's and sick leave.

There is one other matter I wish to refer to. And I am glad to see the Minister of Marine and Fisheries (Mr. Hazen) here, because I shall speak both to him and to the acting Minister of Eail-ways and Canals at the same time. The Eailway Department owns a wharf at Souris, P.E.I. It is the only public wharf in the place. Souris is a harbour of refuge, but the harbour is small. The Government has spent considerable sums, and is spending a large sum this year, to improve the harbour. The entire work has cost hundreds of thousands of dollars, and I have seen as many as thirty or forty sailing vessels, most of them from Nova Scotia, seeking shelter in that harbour at one time. As I have said, the harbour is small and there is not room for all vessels to anchor in the roadstead. The harbourmaster compels vessels to get out of the way of the steamers and tie up to the wharf. During the last couple of years fishermen tying up to the wharf have been obliged to pay side-wharfage, which, in former years they did not have to pay. They feel that this is a hardship. They are willing to pay it if the wharf is needed for commercial purposes at the time. The railway station master at the place has instructions to collect this wharfage; and no doubt it gives him a great deal of trouble, as it amounts to only a dollar or two for each vessel. The fishermen try to avoid payment, because they think it a hardship as they do not have to pay a similar fee at any other port.	Where;
there is a marine wharf it is free to fishermen. I do not suppose the railway collects more than $30 or $40 a year and I am sure it costs more time and energy than it is 
worth. I think some arrangement might be made so that when the wharf is not needed for commercial purposes the fishermen might have the use of it without paying. The Marine Department is spending a great deal of money to develop the fisheries and the Public Works is spending money to make this a harbour of refuge, and it is very annoying for the sake of a trifling sum like this, to cause so much annoyance. Some other arrangement should be made.

I am not justifying the point of view of the commission. I am trying to get before this House such an historic setting to this inquiry that hon. gentlemen will appreciate the conditions that existed. Some of the charges made by Dr. Edwards referred to a date anterior to the five years of the appointment of the commission. The written charges have no dates from which that could be judged, but it was well known to Dr. Edwards and others who were present that his charges went beyond the five years' limit. The commissioners put this construction upon their commission, that if the charge had reference to something that happened more than five years back they would not take it into consideration unless it was shown that it affected the present penitentiary administration. In other words, that if the particular charge under review, although it was over five years old, had no part or bearing on the existing conditions of the penitentiary, they would not consider the charge. Owing to that construction put on the commission, whether it was right or wrong I cannot say, Dr. Edwards and the commissioners came into conflict. It is a matter of record that from time to time the conflict became more acute, and finally the antagonism between the commission and Dr. Edwards was so marked that the newspapers reported the proceedings with double-leaded type, and it was a matter of daily comment in Kingston that one could not tell what conflict might ensue the day following.
I am not- seeking to discuss who is right or who is wrong; but I say it ie unfortunate that this conflict arose at the beginning, because I think it has detracted somewhat from the report of three gentlemen eminently qualified to make a report, which if it has the consideration that it should have by the Minister of Justice, should go a long way towards improving conditions in the penitentiary at Kingston. I think the hon. member for Frontenae had a desire to be fair in what he said to-night;

but I suppose a man may hold a cent so close to his eye that it may blind the sun and I think perhaps the hon. member has attached so much importance to the charges that he made against the deputy warden and the surgeon that he has blinded himself to the beneficial results that might be brought about by this commission, for the appointment of which he was primarily responsible.
